{"name":"Light Steel Blue","hex":"#b0c4de","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"light","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye (about 400–750 nanometers): visible light.","Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye or in nearby ranges (infrared or ultraviolet radiation)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To start (a fire).","To set fire to; to set burning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light"},{"term":"steel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An artificial metal produced from iron, harder and more elastic than elemental iron; used figuratively as a symbol of hardness.","Any item made of this metal, particularly including:\nBladed or pointed weapons, as swords, javelins, daggers."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of steel.","Similar to steel in color, strength, or the like; steely."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/steel"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"X11 color names","blocks":[{"text":"In computing, on the X Window System, X11 color names are represented in a simple text file, which maps certain strings to RGB color values. It was traditionally shipped with every X11 installation, hence the name, and is usually located in <X11root>/lib/X11/rgb.txt. The web colors list is descended from it but differs for certain color names."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X11_color_names","wikidata":"Q376249"},{"title":"Steel blue","blocks":[{"text":"Steel blue is a shade of blue color that resembles blue steel, i.e., steel which has been subjected to bluing for protection from rust. It is one of the less vibrant shades of blue, and is usually identified as a blue-gray color. An archaic term for it is chalybeous."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Niter_and_Color_Case.jpg/330px-Niter_and_Color_Case.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steel_blue","wikidata":"Q1003676"},{"title":"Steel","blocks":[{"text":"Steel is an alloy of iron and carbon that demonstrates improved mechanical properties compared to the pure form of iron. Due to its high elastic modulus, yield strength, fracture strength and low raw material cost, steel is one of the most commonly manufactured materials in the world. Steel is used in structures, in bridges, infrastructure, tools, ships, trains, cars, bicycles, machines, electrical appliances, furniture, and weapons."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/%D0%90%D1%80%D0%BC%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%83%D1%80%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%82.jpg/330px-%D0%90%D1%80%D0%BC%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%83%D1%80%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%82.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steel","wikidata":"Q11427"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light","wikidata":"Q9128"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Shades_of_red.png/500px-Shades_of_red.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Shades_of_red.png/500px-Shades_of_red.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AShades_of_red.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Konstruktionsst%C3%A5l.JPG/500px-Konstruktionsst%C3%A5l.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Konstruktionsst%C3%A5l.JPG/1280px-Konstruktionsst%C3%A5l.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Konstruktionsst%C3%A5l.JPG/500px-Konstruktionsst%C3%A5l.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Structural steel bars of different shapes used in construction","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKonstruktionsst%C3%A5l.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Steel_wire_rope.JPG/500px-Steel_wire_rope.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Steel_wire_rope.JPG/1280px-Steel_wire_rope.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Steel_wire_rope.JPG/500px-Steel_wire_rope.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Steel cable of a colliery headframe","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASteel_wire_rope.JPG"},{"srcset":"http